<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">North West Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North West Company was a fur-trading partnership based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> that competed fiercely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> across western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Its traders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> allies pushed far inland and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a>, a conflict that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seven-oaks">Battle of Seven Oaks</a> in 1816. It merged with the Hudson's Bay Company in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-west-company.jpg" alt="North West Company" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_West_Company_-_Coat_Of_Arms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the British conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in 1763, English speakers took over the management of the fur trading posts, and the high cost of outfitting canoes for the far west pushed these pedlars to combine. The standard histories trace the company to a 16-share organisation of 1779 that included Isaac Todd, Simon McTavish and James McGill. It was put on a long-term footing in the winter of 1783–1784, and in 1787 it absorbed its rival Gregory, McLeod and Co., which brought in Alexander Mackenzie. From then the firm of McTavish, Frobisher and Company effectively controlled it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the company's auspices Mackenzie descended the river now named after him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arctic-ocean">Arctic Ocean</a> in 1789 and crossed overland from Peace River to the Pacific in 1793, while David Thompson and Simon Fraser explored the Rocky Mountains and the Interior Plateau. Dissident partners formed the XY Company, which Mackenzie soon joined; after McTavish died on 6 July 1804 his nephew William McGillivray merged the two firms that year. In 1813 the company bought Fort Astoria from John Jacob Astor's Pacific Fur Company and renamed it Fort George.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>'s charter gave it a virtual monopoly in Rupert's Land, and the rivalry turned violent after Thomas Douglas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-douglas-5th-earl-of-selkirk">Lord Selkirk</a>, obtained the Selkirk Concession. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seven-oaks">Battle of Seven Oaks</a> in 1816 Selkirk arrested McGillivray and other proprietors, seized Fort William and charged them with the deaths of 21 people. Ordered by Henry Bathurst, Secretary of State for War and the Colonies, to stop the hostilities, the partners signed a merger in July 1821, bringing 97 trading posts to the Hudson's Bay Company's 76.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The masters, or bourgeois, were mostly of Scottish descent, often related by blood or marriage: 77 of 128 leading figures were of Scots descent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> agents hired staff, bought goods and exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, while wintering partners, each holding a share or two, ran the inland districts and met the agents every July at Grand Portage on Lake Superior, later at Fort William. Clerks served five to seven years as apprentices in hope of a partnership.</p><p>The servants, or engagés, were mostly Canadiens, peasants' sons from around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Seasonal voyageurs who paddled between Montreal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> were called pork-eaters, and wintering servants worked the interior posts. In 1799 the company employed nearly 2,500 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North West Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Pemmican War, men of the Hudson's Bay Company and the <b>North West Company</b> fought a violent confrontation near modern-day Winnipeg, also called the Seven Oaks Massacre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederacy traded furs at Hudson's Bay Company and <b>North West Company</b> posts but kept American trappers out of its lands for decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The rival <b>North West Company</b> and its Métis allies saw the colony as a threat to the pemmican supply of the fur trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Competition from the <b>North West Company</b> pushed the company inland after 1774, ending in merger in 1821.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a></li></ul></section>
